 {
  "id": "t4_explain_dirty_air",
  "question": "What does the dirty air cost measure, and what does it leave out?",
  "expect": "explanation, no table needed or dirty_air_cost used",
  "calls": [],
  "answer": "The dirty‑air cost measures the total seconds a driver loses in a race because they are stuck in the turbulent wake of the car ahead—essentially the time lost to following another car. It is calculated from telemetry and lap‑time data and ranks drivers by the largest cost first. This metric leaves out other race‑impacting factors such as tire wear, fuel load, driver skill, and mechanical issues, focusing solely on the aerodynamic penalty of dirty air."
 },
